vexyl 1
meta {
  name "International code signal flag A"
  encoder "Naomi Persephone Amethyst"
  license "CC-BY-SA-4.0"
  render-license "CC0-1.0"
  source "https://web.archive.org/web/20260606134814/https://www.dco.uscg.mil/Portals/9/NMC/pdfs/examinations/Pub102_2020.pdf"
  source "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:ICS_Alfa.svg"
  note "From the code: the design, in the plate International Flags and Pennants inside the front cover of the International Code of Signals, United States edition of 1969 as revised in 2020 (NGA Pub. 102), read in the Coast Guard's copy as the Wayback Machine kept it on 6 June 2026. Chapter 1, Section 3, says a set has 26 alphabetical flags, ten numeral pennants, three substitutes and the answering pennant, and the code gives no sizes or shades. The plate draws A as white at the hoist and blue at the fly, with a swallowtail."
  note "From the Commons drawing: the square shape, the swallowtail's depth of a quarter of the length, and the black border that edges the white. The plate draws the flags about 4:5, hoist to fly, and A's swallowtail about a third of the length deep, but it's a small picture, not a construction."
  note "The colors are the Commons drawing's pure primaries. The plate is in red, blue, yellow, black and white, with no shades given."
}
palette {
  white "#ffffff"
  blue "#0000ff"
  black "#000000"
}
proportion hoist=600 fly=600
outline swallowtail {
  depth 150
}
border black 2.5
bars white blue {
  along hoist
}
